3. Measurement Theory of Naturalness

3.1 Naturalness as a Latent Construct

Definition 1 [C]. The naturalness of a sung item is the psychological construct whose observable manifestations are comparative judgements by listeners.

By the representational theory of measurement, a measure is a homomorphism from an empirical relational structure to a numerical one. The minimum admissible structure is ordinal: a function \nu on items with

a\succ b \;\Longleftrightarrow\; \nu(a)>\nu(b).

Anything stronger — an interval scale, hence arithmetic means, hence a cardinal reading of a "naturalness score" — requires an additional argument, and that argument is the business of §3.3.

3.2 The MOS Problem

Consequence [E]. A mean of mean-opinion scores is not a cardinal measure of naturalness. The five-point MOS scale is ordinal; treating it as interval-valued assumes that listeners deploy it as an interval scale, an assumption that is not established for singing evaluation and is routinely violated in practice.

The design therefore proceeds as follows. Forced binary preference is the primary dependent variable. MOS are collected but analysed with cumulative link mixed models, which respect ordinality and accommodate random effects for listeners and items. Ordinal descriptive statistics are reported alongside any model-based estimate, and divergence between the two is reported rather than resolved by preference for the more convenient.

Proportional-odds check [E]. Cumulative link mixed models assume proportional odds across category thresholds. This assumption is testable and is added to the diagnostic protocol of §3.3; a model whose proportional-odds assumption fails does not license the interval interpretation it was introduced to support.

3.3 Preference Models and Diagnostic Protocol

Thurstone Case V [E].

P(a\succ b) \;=\; \Phi\!\left(\frac{\mu_a-\mu_b}{\sigma\sqrt{2}}\right),

with \Phi the standard normal distribution function. To avoid the symbol collision present in earlier versions, the admissibility map of §2.2 is written \Psi and the normal distribution function is written \Phi; the two never share a symbol again.

Bradley–Terry [E].

P(a\succ b) \;=\; \frac{\pi_a}{\pi_a+\pi_b},\qquad \pi_i=e^{\beta_i}.

Diagnostic rationale [E]. Bayesian paired-comparison work reports that the two models give similar estimates on complete data, with Bradley–Terry slightly more credible for quality differences. Divergence between them is therefore a weak diagnostic of interval-scale failure and is no longer used as one. The v0.1 plan to treat divergence as a signal is formally withdrawn.

Diagnostic protocol [C]. Three targeted checks replace the divergence test.

1. Intransitivity against a model-implied null. Intransitivity is endogenous to the data-generating process that Thurstone's model assumes: momentary discriminal dispersion guarantees some circular triads even when the construct is perfectly unidimensional. A fixed threshold therefore cannot distinguish a multidimensional construct from close pairs plus ordinary dispersion. We fit a model with pair-specific error terms — which treats intransitivity as a parameter rather than a failure — and ask, by parametric bootstrap, whether the observed circular-triad rate exceeds the model-implied distribution. Kendall's coefficient of consistency is reported alongside, with the chance rate of 0.25 stated so the reader can calibrate the number.
2. Equal-dispersion check. Case III is fitted and compared with Case V by likelihood-ratio test; rejection requires cautious interpretation of scale values.
3. Ordinal threshold inspection. A graded response model or cumulative link mixed model is fitted to MOS; ordered and well-separated thresholds license approximate interval interpretation for the sample, and nothing more.

3.4 Agreement, Disagreement, and the Status of Reliability Coefficients

Calibration [C]. Inter-rater agreement is reported continuously, not as a gate. Krippendorff's \alpha convention treats 0.800 as supporting conclusions and 0.667–0.800 as supporting tentative conclusions only; a value of 0.667 is therefore the minimum for inferential use, not a comfortable target. But preference data can legitimately show low agreement when listeners genuinely differ, and a low \alpha on a liking dimension is informative rather than disqualifying. The design therefore models listener disagreement as variance in the mixed-effects structure and reports \alpha as a descriptive diagnostic. A hard gate at 0.5, as proposed in the previous version, is withdrawn: it was lax relative to convention and simultaneously too rigid as an inferential rule.

3.5 Consequences for the Protocol

Binary preference is primary. A "no preference" option is offered and excluded from the preference proportion p, but its rate is reported per condition, because that rate is itself evidence about discriminability — a design in which ties are impossible cannot distinguish a population with genuinely opposite preferences from a population with no perceptible difference. Disguised identical pairs supply a listener-screening variable, not a model test. Uncertainty is reported as confidence intervals and effect sizes. The listener sample is declared insufficient for universal validation and conclusions are scoped to sample, stimuli, and playback chain.

