4. Automatic Metrics: Validity Conditions and Replacements

Automatic metrics serve two functions only: diagnosing synthesis-operator failure, and screening candidates before human evaluation. They never support a final claim. Each metric is stated with the condition that must hold before it is used.

4.1 Pitch Fidelity

\mathrm{F0\text{-}RMSE}(u,\hat u)\;=\;\sqrt{\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}_v|}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}_v}\big(F_0(t)-\hat F_0(t)\big)^2},

computed on the voiced support after dynamic-time-warping alignment.

Validity condition [C]. Alignment failure rate \le 10\%. Declared limitation: measures fidelity to the requested trajectory, not naturalness; an excellent score is compatible with a metallic timbre.

4.2 Spectral Distance

d_{\mathrm{mel}}(u,\hat u)\;=\;\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}|}\sum_{t}\big\|\log M_t-\log\hat M_t\big\|_2.

Declared limitation: measures proximity to a reference, not absolute naturalness.

4.3 Intelligibility: Construct-Validity Failure and Replacement

Evidence [E]. Singing-adapted intelligibility indices built for polyphonic mixtures reach Pearson correlation near 0.39 with human intelligibility across a two-hundred-excerpt database, rising to about 0.60 after excluding roughly twelve per cent of excerpts; the excluded cases are driven by breathiness, roughness, hoarseness, pitch, syllable rate, and loudness. The same work reports that a hybrid model combining the index with vocal-specific features reaches about 0.81. Two conclusions follow, and the second is the one usually missed.

1. Construct-validity failure, not correlation deficit. An index that is blind to breathiness cannot adjudicate a system whose central claim concerns breathiness control. The problem is not that the correlation is modest; it is that the metric does not measure the construct.
2. The reference construct is polyphonic. The singing-adapted index treats accompaniment as noise over an extracted vocal reference. For a cappella robot singing that referent does not exist, so condition-stratified use of the same index on solo material re-imports the very construct failure that motivated the replacement. The index is therefore not used for a cappella conditions at all, rather than used with a lower threshold.

Replacement protocol [C].

· Primary: character error rate from a strong multilingual recogniser against the reference lyric.
· Hard gate: the recogniser's error rate must be validated against human transcription, per condition, before it is used for inference. Sung-lyric recognition degrades sharply relative to speech benchmarks, and a speech-derived error rate is not evidence about sung intelligibility. Human transcription on a subset is a required control, not a pilot luxury.
· Reported but non-inferential: any legacy intelligibility index, with condition-stratified correlations, and with an explicit statement that below the declared correlation floor it is descriptive only.

4.4 Voice-Quality Proxies

Cepstral peak prominence and harmonics-to-noise ratio are both carried, not one. Cepstral peak prominence is the more robust correlate of breathiness in sustained phonation, while harmonics-to-noise ratio degrades under the jitter and shimmer that singing legitimately contains. Reporting both, and their disagreement, is part of the competing-explanation analysis of §6.6.

4.5 Latency

Measured from input to first available sample, reported as median and 95th percentile over repeated runs with thermal conditions declared, and rejected if inter-repetition variability exceeds a declared fraction. Latency is a resource constraint, not a perceptual claim.

