5. Optimisation Under Perceptual Uncertainty

5.1 Hierarchical Formulation

Level 1 — style selection.

s^\star \;=\; \arg\min_{s\in\mathcal{S}}\;\Big\{\mathbb{E}\big[L_{\mathrm{nat}}\mid s,\mathcal{D}_n\big]+\beta\,\mathrm{Var}\big[L_{\mathrm{nat}}\mid s,\mathcal{D}_n\big]\Big\},

where \mathcal{D}_n is the accumulated set of human evaluations and \beta an exploration coefficient.

Level 2 — trajectory optimisation, conditional on s^\star.

(\mathbf{c}^\star,D^\star)\;=\;\arg\min_{\mathbf{c},D}\;\sum_{i=1}^{4}\lambda_i L_i(\mathbf{c},D\mid s^\star)\quad\text{subject to}\quad \Psi(s^\star,\mathbf{c},D)\le 0,

with L_1 musical fidelity, L_2 the naturalness surrogate, L_3 intelligibility, and L_4 resource cost.

Why the hierarchy is not cosmetic [C]. Joint optimisation over (s,\mathbf{c},D) allows the optimiser to reduce frame-level loss by collapsing style toward a neutral point. The result is locally coherent and globally stylistically incoherent — precisely the failure the style-conversion literature documents when identity and style are converted jointly rather than sequentially. The hierarchy is the formal response.

5.2 Non-Identifiability and the Canonical-Representative Problem

Conjecture 1 [C]. \mathcal{V} is not injective on \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}}: there exist u\neq u' with \mathcal{V}(u) and \mathcal{V}(u') perceptually indiscernible.

Status of the argument. The previous version labelled this a proposition and offered a proof sketch from positive-dimensional fibres. That sketch is a heuristic, not a proof: continuity on an infinite-dimensional space does not imply non-injectivity, and perceptual indiscernibility is a property of the listener, not of \mathcal{V} alone. The claim is therefore restated as a conjecture, and its demonstration is empirical: exhibit a pair (u,u') driven by optimisation toward perceptual identity, and show by the paired protocol that the difference is below the differential threshold. Until such a pair is exhibited, the claim is a hypothesis about the geometry of \mathcal{V} and the acuity of listeners, and is labelled as such.

The circularity problem. Selecting the canonical representative of a perceptual equivalence class by minimal resource cost requires knowing which elements are perceptually equivalent — that is, requires the perceptual metric the campaign exists to validate. The procedure cannot presuppose its own conclusion.

Two-stage procedure [C].

Stage 1, pre-campaign. Approximate equivalence classes with a perceptual dissimilarity proxy validated on independent human similarity judgements. If no proxy reaches the declared validity floor, do not define a canonical representative; instead return a portfolio of trajectories from the presumptive class and let the preference campaign adjudicate among them.

Stage 2, post-campaign. Only if the campaign confirms indiscernibility within a class is the representative defined by minimal resource cost. If indiscernibility is not confirmed, the notion of canonical representative is refuted for this system and §5.2 is rewritten rather than patched.

5.3 Surrogate Validity and the Primacy of Human-in-the-Loop Optimisation

Naturalness is not computable, so a surrogate \hat L_{\mathrm{nat}} is used — but only under a pre-registered validity condition on held-out preference data.

Calibrated stance [C]. The benchmark evidence on singing naturalness prediction is sobering: the best reported objective metrics correlate with subjective scores at levels well below what a decision-grade surrogate would require, with per-system correlations in the region of 0.4–0.5 and per-utterance correlations substantially lower. Two consequences are adopted rather than lamented.

1. Human-in-the-loop preferential Bayesian optimisation is the primary method, not the fallback. Preference feedback is modelled with a Bradley–Terry likelihood, which makes the optimisation objective formally consistent with the psychometric model of §3.3: the quantity being optimised and the quantity being measured are the same quantity.
2. The surrogate is a search aid and nothing else. It may warm-start and it may screen candidates cheaply. It never supports a claim of the form "the surrogate shows that…". Two thresholds are declared in advance and labelled as engineering conventions rather than literature-derived constants: a lower floor for search use and a higher floor for inference, the latter acknowledged as currently unreachable in the singing domain and therefore included to mark the distance rather than to be met.

A publicly available singing naturalness benchmark with lyric and melody sub-scores supplies the pilot's anchor stimuli and free calibration data; it does not supply a validated surrogate, and it is not treated as one.

5.4 Determinism Contract, Summarised

Functional determinism conditional on the content seed is a theoretical requirement, tested by distributional equality. Numerical insensitivity is the primary robustness claim, tested by a distributional-equivalence probe under permitted numerical variation. Bit-exactness is a release artefact, tested by hash equality under a published environment container, and its failure is an engineering finding rather than a theoretical refutation. The environment is logged and published, never seeded.

