6. Planned Experimental Protocol (Declared, Not Executed)

Statement of non-execution. No part of this section has been carried out. The campaign has not been run; no listener has been recruited; no stimulus has been rendered; no model has been trained. This section specifies the protocol to which the authors will be bound, so that the binding can be inspected before the data exist.

6.1 Hypotheses and the Three-Outcome Falsification Rule

Let p be the proportion of forced-choice preferences for the proposed system over a named comparator, with ties excluded. Let \delta be the smallest effect size of interest, fixed a priori.

Three outcomes, not two [C]. The previous design refuted (T) whenever the proposed system was "not significantly preferred." That is unsound: with limited power, failure to reject the null is not evidence for the null, and a true preference of, say, 0.57 — substantively meaningful — would be recorded as a refutation. The rule is therefore interval-based:

· Conclusive win: the 95\% confidence interval for p lies entirely above 0.5+\delta.
· Equivalence to the comparator: the 95\% confidence interval lies entirely below 0.5+\delta, by a two-one-sided-tests procedure against the equivalence bound.
· Inconclusive: the interval straddles the bound. The honest report is that more data are required, and no claim of refutation is licensed.

Falsification of (T) in strong form. (T) is refuted in its strong form if the margin-matched, structure-destroyed baseline of §6.2 reaches equivalence with the proposed system. In that case the data support only the weak claim that variation per se matters, and the thesis is amended accordingly. An inconclusive outcome refutes nothing and is reported as such.

Other named falsifiers. The measurement framework is refuted if ordinal threshold recovery fails or if intransitivity exceeds the model-implied bootstrap distribution. The surrogate's role is refuted if its held-out correlation falls below the search floor. The determinism contract is refuted if the numerical-insensitivity probe shows a preference-distribution shift. The sufficiency of the four-component space is refuted if voice-quality mediation (§6.6) explains the preference effect.

6.2 Comparators: Three Baselines and One Mandatory Strong System

Mandatory strong open comparator [C]. An open, technique-controllable singing synthesis system is included as a required comparator, not an optional upper bound. The reason is straightforward: the style-conversion literature shows that top systems approach human naturalness on identity and fall short mainly on dynamics, so a mechanical baseline alone would allow a win against a caricature to be mistaken for evidence about (T). The strong comparator makes the claim "a controlled voice can beat an expensive voice" testable instead of rhetorical.

Baseline 1 — mechanical. Sustained notes, no humanisation. Lower bound and calibration stimulus. Necessary, and explicitly insufficient alone.

Baseline 2 — margin-matched, structure-destroyed. This is the operational test of (T) [C]. The baseline must differ from the proposed system only in joint dependence, not in marginal statistics. Two admissible constructions are specified:

· Copula construction. Estimate the marginal distribution of each dynamic component from the proposed system's own output; resample trajectories from a copula with the same marginals and an independence (or fitted-parameter) dependence structure, thereby destroying cross-component coupling while preserving each component's law.
· Surrogate construction. Generate iterated amplitude-adjusted Fourier transform surrogates, which preserve both the power spectrum and the marginal amplitude distribution while destroying nonlinear temporal structure.

Mandatory pre-registration for Baseline 2. Marginal equivalence between the proposed system and the baseline is tested by Kolmogorov–Smirnov and Anderson–Darling tests per component, with the acceptance criteria declared before generation, and the generator seed published. A structure-destroyed baseline whose marginals differ is not a test of (T); it is a second mechanical baseline in disguise. This is the single most consequential design decision in the volume.

Baseline 3 — technique-aware but weakly coupled. Technique labels applied independently of the joint constraints of §2.2. This baseline isolates whether any technique conditioning helps from whether the specific joint constraints help.

Dose–response extension [C]. Because the binary contrast discards information, the coupling functional \kappa of §2.2 permits a graded design: coupling strength is set to a declared ladder of levels — including zero, intermediate values, unity, and an over-coupled level beyond the admissible region's interior — and the preference curve is estimated across levels. A monotone or inverted-U curve is a sharper test of (T) than a single contrast, because it predicts a specific shape rather than merely a sign. The over-coupled level is included deliberately: if naturalness decreases when coupling is pushed beyond the corpus-implied range, the thesis gains a quantitative boundary rather than a qualitative slogan.

Loudness equalisation [C], declared as a protocol requirement. Equalisation is applied to both playback chains — robot actuator and headphones — using objective level calibration at the listening position, with the perceptual target tolerance declared in advance as a pre-registered convention. The value is a convention, not a literature-mandated perceptual threshold; what the literature does establish is that reproduction level drives quality judgements and that unmatched levels produce a systematic bias toward the second stimulus in paired presentation. Within-pair level mismatch is therefore entered in the threat table with a measured mitigation, not merely acknowledged.

Playback and embodiment as declared factors [C]. The playback chain (robot actuator versus headphones) is a fixed factor, and an audio-only versus audio-plus-embodiment factor is declared where the platform permits it. This is a precautionary control. Robot singing has a documented lineage — voice and facial motion imitation on a humanoid platform, avatar-driven robotic singing with lip-sync, and commercial humanoid performance — and the human–robot interaction evidence indicates that humanlike voices are judged less eerie and more likable than mechanical ones, which makes any "robotic timbre is preferred" claim uphill rather than default. The embodiment factor is included to test for a reversal, and the result is reported whichever way it falls.

6.3 Pilot (Planned)

A pilot with a small listener panel and a small item set precedes the campaign. It includes the three baselines and the strong comparator, at least one high-breathiness item and one high-vibrato item, the playback and embodiment factor, and at least one Arabic-ornamented item. Its functions are: to calibrate the inconsistency prior for identical-pair screening against documented ranges rather than an asserted constant; to supply realistic effect sizes for the per-contrast power simulation; to test the determinism and insensitivity contracts on the campaign hardware; and to decide whether the four-component space is already insufficient before the main campaign commits to it.

Arabic probe, scoped honestly [C]. One item supports no inference. It is a descriptive probe capable of detecting catastrophic failure and nothing more. Any Arabic inference requires a stratified native-listener sub-panel and several items. The formal framework does accommodate the material — pitch is carried in cents, so microtonal targets are representable; the pitch-velocity bound of §2.2 is relaxed for tahrir-type melisma rather than inherited from speech; and the coupling constraints estimated on Western material are explicitly not assumed to transfer, since maqam intonation includes constitutive neutral intervals and prescribed per-mode ornament rather than decorative variation. Public Arabic singing resources suitable for estimating coupling constraints are limited, so the protocol budgets for in-house labelled recording rather than assuming a corpus exists.

6.4 Power Analysis by Simulation, Per Contrast

Closed-form power formulas are unreliable for the planned hierarchical model, so power is estimated by simulation. Critically, the simulation is run separately for each confirmatory contrast at that contrast's own smallest effect of interest, because the smallest expected effect belongs to the margin-matched baseline, and a single simulation calibrated on the largest expected effect would overstate power for the comparison that carries the thesis.

The procedure: simulate preference data under the alternative with crossed listener and item random effects and condition slopes; fit the planned model; estimate the rejection rate over a large number of replications; increase listeners or items if simulated power falls below the conventional target; and publish the simulation specification in advance. Expectation stated plainly: the nominal observation count is inflated by clustering, so the effective sample is smaller than the nominal one, and the design must be sized on the effective sample. Documented field norms — a substantial majority of listening-test papers historically used fewer than twenty listeners — are context, not justification.

Clustering structure. Random effects are crossed for listeners and items, with condition slopes for each. With a small item set, an item random slope is estimated from too few levels to be credible; the protocol therefore either raises the item count to a level at which the slope is defensible or models item as fixed and scopes the conclusions accordingly.

6.5 Analysis Plan and Multiplicity

Preference proportions with bootstrap confidence intervals stratified by listener; Bradley–Terry mixed-effects models with crossed random effects; Thurstone Case V and Case III fits for the dispersion diagnostic; cumulative link mixed models for MOS with proportional-odds and threshold inspection; intransitivity against the model-implied bootstrap distribution with Kendall's consistency reported; order and fatigue effects tested; every hypothesis reported including non-significant ones.

Multiplicity [C]. Three confirmatory contrasts are planned, so family-wise error is controlled by a step-down procedure across them, and the confirmatory family is pre-registered as a family. Exploratory analyses are declared exploratory and are not used to support (T).

6.6 Pre-Declared Competing Explanation: Voice Quality

The technique-controllable synthesis literature treats breathy tone, falsetto, mixed voice, and vocal fry as first-class dimensions, not as epiphenomena of frame-level pitch, energy, and breathiness trajectories. If the proposed system fails to reach a conclusive win over the margin-matched baseline, the most plausible explanation is a voice-quality deficit outside the four-component space. This explanation is declared before the campaign so that a null can be interpreted rather than merely reported.

Measurement, and the limits of the analysis. Cepstral peak prominence and harmonics-to-noise ratio are measured, with independent perceptual breathiness and roughness ratings on a subset. The analysis is framed as pre-registered covariate adjustment and competing-explanation comparison, not as formal causal mediation: a mediation analysis at the planned sample size would be underpowered and its indirect-effect estimate would carry more inferential weight than the design can support. The question asked is the weaker but answerable one — does the preference effect persist after adjustment for measured voice quality, and does the pattern of disagreement between the two acoustic proxies point to a quality dimension the control space does not carry?

