Appendix C — Synthesis Operator, Determinism, and Numerical Insensitivity

C.1 Functional Determinism, Formally

Definition [C]. \mathcal{V} is functionally deterministic if, for every u\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{adm}} and every seed \sigma, the law of \mathcal{V}(u;\sigma) is a point mass:

\Pr\big[\mathcal{V}(u;\sigma)=y\big]=1 \quad\text{for a single } y\in L^2([0,T]).

Test. Render the same (u,\sigma) R times; compute pairwise sample-wise maximum absolute difference on the waveform. Functional determinism is supported if this quantity is exactly zero on the declared environment.

C.2 Seeding

\sigma=\mathrm{hash}\big(\text{lyrics}\,\|\,\text{melody}\,\|\,\text{style\_id}\big).

Content-only rule [C]. The environment does not enter the seed. Rationale: an environment-dependent seed would make outputs differ across machines by construction, which defeats the purpose of a reproducibility contract. The environment is recorded in a separate manifest (C.5) and published.

C.3 Numerical Insensitivity Probe (Primary Robustness Claim)

The scientifically relevant claim is not bit-exactness but invariance of the preference distribution under permitted numerical variation. The probe is:

1. Define a set \mathcal{E}=\{e_1,\dots,e_m\} of permitted execution variants: deterministic flags on/off where both are valid, tensor-float matmul enabled/disabled, two valid kernel algorithm selections, two valid reduction orders.
2. Render the stimulus set under each variant. Where a variant produces a waveform hash difference, confirm the difference is below a declared spectral tolerance (so that the variant is a numerical variant, not a content variant).
3. Run the paired protocol (§E) on a subset under the reference variant and under the extreme variants.
4. Test distributional equivalence of the preference outcome across variants.

Interpretation rule. A shift in the preference distribution under a permitted numerical variant refutes the determinism contract as stated. A mere hash difference does not, provided step 2 passed.

C.4 Bit-Exact Release Contract (Secondary)

For the frozen stimulus set:

1. Render on the campaign hardware.
2. Hash every file (content hash) and publish the hash manifest.
3. Publish the environment manifest.
4. Re-render on the same environment after a declared interval; require hash equality.

A failure here is an engineering finding about the release artefact. It is reported, and it does not refute the theory, because listeners hear frozen files.

C.5 Environment Manifest Fields

The manifest records, at minimum: GPU model and count; driver version; CUDA and cuDNN versions; deep-learning framework version; deterministic-algorithm flag state; tensor-float matmul state; cuBLAS workspace configuration; reduction-order policy; container image digest; operating system; and the exact commands used. Every field is declared before the campaign so that an undocumented change is detectable.

C.6 Engineering Conditions That Must Be Named

The contract is invalid if any of the following is omitted, because each is a documented silent source of run-to-run variation on current accelerators: tensor-float matmul left enabled by default on recent architectures; cuBLAS workspace left unconfigured; deterministic algorithm flags not enabled; reduction order left dependent on thread scheduling; and parallel atomic accumulation left unconstrained.

