Appendix D — Psychometric Derivations and Diagnostics

D.1 Thurstone Case V, Derived

Let each item a have a latent value \mu_a and let a listener's momentary evaluation be X_a=\mu_a+\varepsilon_a with \varepsilon_a\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2), independent across items. The listener prefers a to b iff X_a>X_b. The difference is normal with mean \mu_a-\mu_b and variance 2\sigma^2, hence

P(a\succ b)=\Phi\!\left(\frac{\mu_a-\mu_b}{\sigma\sqrt2}\right).

Corollary. The scale is identified only up to an affine transformation; fixing \sigma=1 and one anchor item fixes the scale. This is why a "naturalness score" is not an absolute quantity and why arithmetic means of raw ratings are not licensed by this model alone.

D.2 Thurstone Case III (Unequal Dispersion)

P(a\succ b)=\Phi\!\left(\frac{\mu_a-\mu_b}{\sqrt{\sigma_a^2+\sigma_b^2}}\right).

The equal-dispersion hypothesis is H_0:\sigma_a=\sigma_b for all pairs. It is tested by likelihood ratio between Case III and Case V fits, with the degrees of freedom equal to the number of distinct dispersion parameters minus one.

D.3 Bradley–Terry as a Logistic Model

P(a\succ b)=\frac{e^{\beta_a}}{e^{\beta_a}+e^{\beta_b}}=\mathrm{logit}^{-1}(\beta_a-\beta_b).

This makes the model estimable by logistic regression on a design matrix with +1 for the first item and -1 for the second in each pair, with a random intercept per listener and a random effect per item. The equivalence with a paired-comparison formulation is exact.

D.4 Graded Response Model for MOS

For an ordinal response Y\in\{1,\dots,K\} with latent trait \theta,

P(Y\ge k\mid\theta)=\Phi\big(a\theta-b_k\big),\qquad k=2,\dots,K,

so that

P(Y=k\mid\theta)=\Phi(a\theta-b_k)-\Phi(a\theta-b_{k-1}),

with b_1=-\infty and b_K=+\infty. Ordered, well-separated thresholds b_k license approximate interval interpretation for the sample; crossed or collapsed thresholds do not.

D.5 Proportional-Odds Check for CLMM

The cumulative link mixed model assumes

\mathrm{logit}\big[P(Y\le k\mid \eta)\big]=\alpha_k-\eta,

with a single linear predictor \eta across categories. The assumption is tested by fitting a model with category-specific slopes and comparing by likelihood ratio, and by inspecting category-specific residual patterns. Failure is reported and the interval interpretation is withheld.

D.6 Intransitivity: Indices and the Model-Implied Null

Circular triads. For n items, let c be the number of triples (a,b,c) with a\succ b, b\succ c, c\succ a. Kendall's coefficient of consistency is

\zeta=1-\frac{c}{c_{\max}},\qquad
c_{\max}=\begin{cases}\dfrac{n^3-4n}{24}, & n \text{ even},\\[6pt] \dfrac{n^3-n}{24}, & n \text{ odd}.\end{cases}

The chance rate for a random preference pattern corresponds to \zeta near zero, and \zeta=1 corresponds to a perfectly transitive order.

Why a fixed threshold is invalid [C]. Under Thurstone's own model, discriminal dispersion produces circular triads by construction for close pairs. A fixed cutoff therefore confounds "multidimensional construct" with "close pairs plus ordinary noise."

Parametric bootstrap procedure.

1. Fit a model with pair-specific error terms (a Thurstonian IRT or a BT model augmented with pair effects), which treats intransitivity as a parameter.
2. Simulate B replicate datasets from the fitted model.
3. For each replicate, compute c.
4. Form the model-implied distribution of c.
5. Report the observed c as a quantile of that distribution.

The framework is refuted only if the observed c lies beyond a declared upper quantile of the model-implied distribution — not if it merely exceeds a fixed number.

D.7 "No Preference" as Diagnostic

Let q be the rate of "no preference" responses per condition. Under a model in which discrimination is imperfect, q is a monotone decreasing function of the discriminability of the pair. The rate is therefore reported per condition as evidence about discriminability, and it is excluded from p. A design without ties cannot separate a population with opposite preferences from a population with no perceptible difference; the tie option restores that separation.

