E.1 Measurement Protocol Details

E.1.1 Task

On each trial the listener hears two stimuli (A, B) and selects one of three responses: prefer A, prefer B, no preference. The binary proportion p is computed on the first two responses only.

E.1.2 Timing

Parameter Declared value Register
Inter-stimulus interval 500 ms [C]
Inter-trial interval 1.5–2.0 s, listener-paced with a minimum [C]
Maximum session length 20 min [C]
Blocks per session 4–6, with declared breaks [C]
Replay allowance one replay per trial [C]

Rationale. The inter-stimulus interval is kept short because a long interval increases memory load and the influence of the first stimulus; a short interval is associated with the "stronger second" bias, which is why the order effect is tested explicitly (§E.4) rather than assumed absent.

E.1.3 Instructions

Instructions are scripted and identical across conditions. They state that the task concerns preference, that there is no correct answer, that "no preference" is a legitimate response, and that the listener may replay once. The script is published with the stimulus set.

E.1.4 Screening

Disguised identical pairs are embedded. A listener is flagged if their "preference" rate on identical pairs exceeds a threshold anchored to documented screening ranges and calibrated by the pilot. Flagged listeners are excluded from the primary analysis, and the exclusion is reported with its rate. The threshold is condition-specific, because loudness-equalised hard conditions are documented to push inconsistency higher than easy conditions.

E.1.5 Loudness Equalisation

Objective calibration. Each stimulus is measured at the listening position with a calibrated sound-level meter, using the item's own programme material rather than a test tone, and level-adjusted so that the declared target is met within the declared tolerance on both playback chains (robot actuator and headphones).

Perceptual check. A small expert panel performs an adaptive loudness-matching task on a subset. Any pair whose matched level differs from the objective calibration by more than the declared tolerance is re-calibrated.

Tolerance. The tolerance is a pre-registered convention [C], not a literature-mandated perceptual threshold. The literature establishes that reproduction level drives quality judgements and that unmatched levels bias paired preference; it does not fix a universal tolerance. The value is declared before the pilot and is not adjusted after seeing preference data.

Drift check. Level is re-measured at the start, middle, and end of each session to detect thermal or gain drift.

E.1.6 Randomisation

Order within pair is randomised with a balanced schedule (equal numbers of A-first and B-first per condition per listener), and trial order within block is randomised under the constraint that no condition repeats more than twice consecutively.

E.1.7 Playback and Embodiment Factors

The playback chain is a fixed factor with two levels (robot actuator, headphones). Where the platform permits, an embodiment factor with two levels (audio-only, audio-plus-embodied-motion) is added. Both factors are declared before the campaign, and both are tested for preference reversal rather than assumed neutral.

E.1.8 Ethics and Data Protection

Informed consent is obtained in writing; the right to withdraw without explanation is stated; audio is not retained beyond the anonymised response record; listener identifiers are replaced by pseudonyms at ingestion; the linkage table is held separately and destroyed at a declared date; and the protocol is submitted for institutional review before any listener is recruited.

