Appendix H — Optimisation Details

H.1 Preferential Bayesian Optimisation

The objective is the latent naturalness L_{\mathrm{nat}}. Observations are pairwise preferences (a\succ b). The likelihood is Bradley–Terry:

P(a\succ b\mid f)=\mathrm{logit}^{-1}\big(f(a)-f(b)\big),

with f a Gaussian-process prior over the control space. The acquisition function is the expected improvement under the preference likelihood (or an information-theoretic alternative), with the exploration coefficient \beta declared before the campaign.

H.2 Surrogate Validity Protocol

1. Split human preference data into a fitting set and a held-out set.
2. Compute the correlation \rho between the surrogate and held-out preference on the held-out set.
3. Compare \rho to the two pre-registered floors.
4. Decide the mode: surrogate-guided search, or pure human-in-the-loop.

Declared floors [C]. \rho_0^{\mathrm{search}} and \rho_0^{\mathrm{inference}} are engineering conventions, not literature-derived constants. The inference floor is deliberately set above what current singing naturalness predictors achieve; it is included to mark the distance, and no claim of the form "the surrogate shows that…" is licensed until it is met.

H.3 Portfolio Procedure (Fallback for §5.2)

If no dissimilarity proxy reaches its validity floor, the canonical representative is not defined. Instead, a portfolio of trajectories from the presumptive class is returned and the campaign adjudicates. The portfolio size and selection rule are declared before generation.

H.4 Reproducibility of the Optimiser

The optimiser's own stochastic components (initial design points, acquisition tie-breaking) are seeded from the content hash of the item. The initial design, acquisition function, and stopping rule are recorded so that a rerun on the same environment reproduces the trajectory of queries, even if the final selection is adjudicated by humans.

