Appendix I — Metric Definitions and Validity Gates

I.1 Pitch Fidelity

\mathrm{F0\text{-}RMSE}(u,\hat u)=\sqrt{\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}_v|}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}_v}\big(F_0(t)-\hat F_0(t)\big)^2}.

Alignment by dynamic time warping on the voiced support. Gate: alignment failure rate \le 10\%. Limitation: fidelity to the request, not naturalness.

I.2 Spectral Distance

d_{\mathrm{mel}}(u,\hat u)=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}|}\sum_{t}\big\|\log M_t-\log\hat M_t\big\|_2 .

Limitation: proximity to a reference, not absolute naturalness.

I.3 Intelligibility

Primary: character error rate from a strong multilingual recogniser against the reference lyric, after lyric normalisation (case folding, punctuation removal, numeral verbalisation).

Hard gate: per-condition validation against human transcription. The gate is passed only if the recogniser's error rate tracks human transcription within a declared tolerance in each condition, including the high-breathiness condition. A pooled correlation is insufficient.

Secondary, non-inferential: any legacy intelligibility index, reported with condition-stratified correlations and with an explicit statement that it is descriptive only for a cappella conditions, because the reference construct of such indices is polyphonic.

I.4 Voice-Quality Proxies

Cepstral peak prominence is computed on sustained voiced segments with a declared frame length, window, and smoothing. Harmonics-to-noise ratio is computed with a declared pitch-adaptive method. Both are reported with their disagreement, and both enter the covariate-adjustment analysis of §6.6. Neither is treated as a ground truth for breathiness.

I.5 Latency

L_{\mathrm{lat}}=t_{\mathrm{first\ sample}}-t_{\mathrm{input}}.

Median and 95th percentile over repeated runs, with thermal conditions declared. Reject and repeat if inter-repetition variability exceeds a declared fraction.

I.6 Metric Gate Summary

Metric Gate Action if gate fails
