Appendix K — Power Simulation Specification

K.1 Algorithm

1. Fix the design: I listeners, J items, K conditions, R repetitions.
2. Fix the data-generating parameters: marginal preference p per contrast, listener ICC \tau_u^2, item ICC \tau_v^2, condition-slope variance.
3. For b=1,\dots,B:
   (a) draw random effects;
   (b) draw preferences from the BT model;
   (c) fit the planned analysis model;
   (d) apply the three-outcome rule;
   (e) record the outcome.
4. Estimate the rates of conclusive win, equivalence, and inconclusive.

K.2 Scenarios

Scenario p Purpose
Null 0.5 Calibrate false-positive rate of the conclusive-win rule
Weak 0.55 Test whether small true effects are detected
Target 0.65 Primary design target
Strong 0.75 Upper bound for the strong comparator
Equivalence 0.58 Calibrate the equivalence rule against \delta

K.3 Per-Contrast Sizing

The simulation is run separately for each confirmatory contrast at that contrast's own SESOI, because the smallest expected effect belongs to the margin-matched baseline. Sizing on the largest expected effect would overstate power for the comparison that carries the thesis.

K.4 Stopping Rule

The design is accepted if the simulated rate of conclusive win under the target scenario exceeds the conventional target and the simulated rate of conclusive win under the null is at or below the nominal level. If the item count is insufficient for a random slope, the simulation is run with item as a fixed effect and the conclusion is scoped accordingly.

K.5 Pre-Registration Content

The pre-registration fixes: the design dimensions; the data-generating parameters; the number of replications; the analysis model; the three-outcome rule; the SESOI per contrast; the multiplicity procedure; the exclusion rules; and the simulation code identifier.

