Appendix M — Arabic Ornamentation Probe

M.1 Why a Probe and Not a Test

A single ornamented item supports no inference. It is capable of detecting catastrophic failure — a synthesis that cannot represent microtonal targets or that clips ornamented transitions — and nothing more.

M.2 Representation

Pitch is carried in cents, so microtonal targets are representable without a change of representation. The admissible region's pitch bounds are item-specific and accommodate the maqam's ambitus. The pitch-velocity bound v_{\max} is relaxed for ornamented segments (per B.4), rather than inherited from speech or from sustained-note statistics.

M.3 Constraint Transfer

Coupling constraints estimated on Western material are explicitly not assumed to transfer. Arabic maqam intonation includes constitutive neutral intervals and per-mode prescribed ornament, not decorative variation. A transferred constraint would therefore mis-specify the coupling.

M.4 Panel and Items

Any Arabic inference requires a stratified native-listener sub-panel and several items, not one. The probe is reported descriptively, with the item, the listener profile, and the observed behaviour, and with an explicit statement that no generalisation is claimed.

M.5 Corpus Reality

Public resources suitable for estimating coupling constraints for Arabic singing are limited; the relevant resources are predominantly speech or vocal-improvisation material. The protocol therefore budgets for in-house labelled recording rather than assuming a suitable corpus exists.

