Appendix N — Competing-Explanation Analysis (Voice Quality)

N.1 Question

Does the preference effect persist after adjustment for measured voice quality, and do the two acoustic proxies agree on the direction of any residual?

N.2 Measures

Cepstral peak prominence on sustained voiced segments; harmonics-to-noise ratio with a pitch-adaptive method; independent perceptual breathiness and roughness ratings on a subset.

N.3 Analysis

A pre-registered covariate-adjustment model adds the voice-quality measures to the primary model. The reported quantity is the adjusted contrast with its interval. This is not a formal causal mediation analysis: at the planned sample size, an indirect-effect estimate would carry more inferential weight than the design supports. The question asked is the weaker, answerable one.

N.4 Decision Rule

If the adjusted contrast falls to equivalence while the unadjusted contrast was a conclusive win, and if both proxies agree on the direction, the sufficiency of the four-component space is treated as refuted for this system, and the thesis is amended to include voice quality as an additional control dimension.

